//! English explanations of the DNS Query Log window (G4.1zd), scope `dns_query_log`, sorted by
//! key.

/// Explanations.
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("dns_query_log.answered_by", "## Answered by\nWho answered: the cache, an upstream server, a stale answer (RFC 8767), a static entry, the adlist, a router zone, a special name, or refused (outside Allowed From, `limit`, `busy`)."),
    ("dns_query_log.clear", "## Clear\nDeletes from the disk: one client's queries (right to erasure, LGPD art. 18), those older than N hours, or everything. Affected files are rewritten without them. In the journal. On flash, the old block only really goes when the disk reuses it (the ext4 of `/config` does not promise an immediate TRIM)."),
    ("dns_query_log.client", "## Client\nWho asked, as `Client Address` says: the whole address, its network (`100.64.12.0/24`, `2804:…::/56` truncated), a hash `h:…` or nothing. As a filter: an address, a network or `h:<hash>`; an address finds the truncated network holding it."),
    ("dns_query_log.client_address", "## Client Address\nHow a client is written:\n\n- **Truncated** (default): its network only, by the prefixes below (RFC 6235 §4.1.1);\n- **Hashed**: a keyed hash (SipHash) whose key lives in memory and changes every `Salt Rotation`;\n- **None**: no client;\n- **Full address**: the whole address (weighs most under the law)."),
    ("dns_query_log.enabled", "## Enabled\nWrites the queries the LAN forwarder answers. Off by default; turning it on needs the acknowledgement (the command takes `acknowledge=marco-civil-art14`). On and off go to the router's log. Retention still applies when off: what was written goes by age and size."),
    ("dns_query_log.estimate", "## Estimate\nFrom the last minute's queries per second and the bytes per query (12.3 measured, or what the log wrote): GB a day, the hours the ceiling holds, and disk wear a year (write amplification 1.2, **estimado**). A 256 GB SSD (~150 TBW) at 20 000 queries/s lasts ~16 years; an 8 GB eMMC, months. On small flash, keep the ceiling low."),
    ("dns_query_log.except_from", "## Except From\nClients in these networks are never logged."),
    ("dns_query_log.exclude", "## Exclude\nNames never logged, with everything under them (`example.com` also drops `www.example.com`). Comma-separated."),
    ("dns_query_log.export", "## Export\nThe queries the filters show (up to 100 000 at a time) as CSV or JSON lines, saved to Downloads. The export is in the journal with the user and the filters: personal data leaving the router."),
    ("dns_query_log.latency", "## ms\nFrom the packet read to the reply sent. A cache hit takes under 0.1 ms; an upstream, tens."),
    ("dns_query_log.law", "## Marco Civil and LGPD\nAt a connection provider, keeping which subscriber asked which name and when looks like an **application access record**, which Marco Civil da Internet (Lei 12.965/2014) **art. 14 forbids** connection providers to keep (art. 13 asks only for connection records, 1 year). Under the LGPD (Lei 13.709/2018) the log is personal data: keep the minimum (art. 6 III), delete it when done (art. 16), protect it (art. 46).\n\nWhat the router does by default: off; turning it on takes acknowledging this (in the journal with the user and time); clients **truncated** (/24 and /56); a disk ceiling and 24 h of age; delete per client (art. 18) and export by command.\n\nWith `hashed`, the hash keyed in memory is **pseudonymous** (LGPD art. 13 §4) while the key lives; after rotation not even the router links it back. Turning it on is the operator's call."),
    ("dns_query_log.max_age", "## Max Age\nHours a query is kept (default 24). Older ones go, with the log on or off."),
    ("dns_query_log.max_size", "## Max Size\nThe log's disk ceiling in MiB, also capped at 20 % of `/config`. Full, the oldest hour goes. At 20 000 queries/s the log writes ~21 GB a day (**medido**: ~12 bytes a query): the ceiling, not the days, decides what stays. Before each write, what the next config save needs stays free."),
    ("dns_query_log.name", "## Name\nThe name asked, lowercase. As a filter, `*.example.com` finds it and everything under it; other text finds names containing it. In parentheses, the extended error (RFC 8914) the reply stands for."),
    ("dns_query_log.only_from", "## Only From\nOnly clients in these networks are logged (empty: all). E.g. just the management network."),
    ("dns_query_log.prefixes", "## Prefixes\nBits kept by `Truncated`: 24 for IPv4 (AdGuard Home keeps 16) and 56 for IPv6, a subscriber's usual delegated prefix (not the device). Behind CGNAT a /24 groups ~250 subscribers."),
    ("dns_query_log.rcode", "## Rcode\nThe response code (RFC 1035 §4.1.1): NOERROR, NXDOMAIN (no such name), SERVFAIL, REFUSED. `dropped`: no reply went out (over `max-queries-per-client` over UDP)."),
    ("dns_query_log.salt_rotation", "## Salt Rotation\nWith `Hashed`, how many hours one key is used. The key never reaches the disk: after the change nobody, the router included, links an old hash to an address."),
    ("dns_query_log.time", "## Time (UTC)\nWhen the reply went out, UTC, with milliseconds. Searches go newest to oldest."),
    ("dns_query_log.type", "## Type\nThe record type asked (A, AAAA, HTTPS, PTR…). As a filter, its name or `TYPE65`."),
    ("dns_query_log.window", "## DNS Query Log\nThe queries the LAN DNS forwarder answered (or dropped), kept on the router's disk: time, client, name, type, response code, who answered and how long it took. Off by default. Files in `/config/dns-log/`, a compressed binary format (~12 bytes a query), with a size and an age ceiling. Commands: `ip.dns.log.set`, `get`, `print`, `export`, `clear`."),
];
